## Problem
Most people moving into AI governance have no public body of work, no place a
hiring manager or prospective client can go to see how they actually think. A
LinkedIn profile lists claims; it doesn't show the work. I wanted a single home
at a URL I control, that ranks under my name, and that presents each project with a 
consistent, professional structure rather than as scattered links.

## Approach
- Built with **Jekyll on GitHub Pages** — free, fast, and version-controlled, so
  the site's own history is visible evidence of steady output.
- Used the **Minimal Mistakes** theme rather than hand-coding, keeping the focus
  on content and structure instead of front-end work.
- Standardized every project on one **Problem / Approach / Deliverable /
  Reflection** case-study structure. Predictable structure is what makes a
  portfolio read as designed rather than assembled.
- Framed the whole site around authority and demonstrated expertise,  a public
  record of work — rather than an active job search.

## Deliverable
- [Visit the site](https://hela86.github.io)
- [View on GitHub](https://github.com/hela86/hela86.github.io)

The site includes a home page, a projects index, per-project case studies, a
certifications page, and an about page — each a version-controlled Markdown file.

## Reflection
The most useful thing this project forced was version control. I had never used
Git before building it; a week later, committing and pushing changes was routine
— and a populated GitHub profile is itself a signal most governance candidates
don't have. The second lesson was that consistency beats polish: once every case
study followed the same four-part structure, the whole portfolio felt more
credible than any single page did on its own. In v2, I want to add a short writing
section that connects to my longer-form work, and tighten the home page so the
90-second read — name, focus, three projects, contact — is even faster.
